        "contenuto_esteso": "By AFP Published On 23 Jun 2026 23 Jun 2026 Two-time NBA Most Valuable Player Giannis Antetokounmpo is joining the Miami Heat from the Milwaukee Bucks, multiple US media outlets, including ESPN, reported on Monday. Miami won a battle with the Boston Celtics for the signature of the 31-year-old Greek, a 10-time All-Star who won a championship with the Bucks in 2021. Recommended Stories list of 3 items list 1 of 3 Last one, the best one? How Lionel Messi keeps doing it at the World Cup list 2 of 3 ‘Living the dream’: Iraqis proud of team despite World Cup loss to France list 3 of 3 World Cup knockouts: Who has made it to the round of 32 stage? end of list ESPN reported that Miami will acquire Antetokounmpo and Bobby Portis in a deal that sends Tyler Herro, Kel’el Ware, Jaime Jaquez Jr and Kasparas Jakucionis in the other direction. Milwaukee will also receive three first-round picks, a pick swap and a second-round pick, ESPN reported. Antetokounmpo’s exit ends more than a year of uncertainty over whether he would finish his career in Milwaukee, the only franchise he has played for since being drafted in 2013. The Greek star had openly vented frustration with the team’s management for not clearing him to play after suffering a left knee hyperextension. Antetokounmpo insisted he was fit and ready to help force a playoff push despite being declared unavailable by Bucks’ front office. Speaking in April after Milwaukee failed to reach the playoffs at the end of a 32-50 season, Antetokounmpo also vented at the team’s failure to build a roster capable of contending for championships. “I feel like sometimes people just don’t listen,” Antetokounmpo said. “They listen to the sources. The main source is me. It is what it is.” Advertisement"
